export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"print3d","name":"3D Print","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"print-stage","scope":"addon:print3d","title":"Print stage: put your model on the printer's bed","summary":"Lay IFC elements of your model on a printer bed, as a scale model or life size, lay them flat, arrange them and send them to the printer without exporting.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["3d print","printer","bed","stage","slicer","g-code","send to printer","scale model","ifc","elements","lay flat","arrange"],"headings":[{"id":"print-part-of-your-model-from-here","text":"Print part of your model from here","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"print-part-of-your-model-from-here\"\u003EPrint part of your model from here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the elements you want in the 3D view (a wall, a column, a stair), then open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint stage\u003C/span\u003E tab in the right panel, or press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint stage\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The model steps aside and a dashed bed appears with your elements on it. They are copies of what the 3D view draws, in the places the model gives them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake the selected elements\u003C/span\u003E reads the 3D view&#039;s selection again; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake everything shown\u003C/span\u003E takes every element the view shows (at most 300 at a time).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements are life size, so most are far bigger than a bed. Type a number in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale 1 :\u003C/span\u003E (50 makes a 1 to 50 model) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake a scale model\u003C/span\u003E: everything shrinks by that one number, so the proportions stay. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELife size\u003C/span\u003E puts it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an element in the list, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELay flat\u003C/span\u003E puts the largest flat face that can carry it on the bed, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/span\u003E turns it a quarter turn and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs designed\u003C/span\u003E puts it back the way the model has it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo layout\u003C/span\u003E takes the last change back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange on the bed\u003C/span\u003E spreads everything out. Choose material and quality, connect the printer once, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to 3D printer\u003C/span\u003E. A box says the time, material and temperatures first; nothing is sent until you say yes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is a copy: moving things on the bed never changes your model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn element that is not a closed solid (its skin has holes, it passes through itself, or the model gives it no shape) is left off the bed and named with the reason, because a slicer would print it wrongly or not at all. Doors, windows and walls made of several pieces are judged as the one element they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew in this build, and nothing has yet been printed on a real printer from BimCraft: watch the first prints. A scale model of a building is thin at small scales: look at the preview for walls thinner than your nozzle.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Print part of your model from here Pick the elements you want in the 3D view (a wall, a column, a stair), then open the Print stage tab in the right panel, or press Print stage in the toolbar. The model steps aside and a dashed bed appears with your elements on it. They are copies of what the 3D view draws, in the places the model gives them. Take the selected elements reads the 3D view's selection again; Take everything shown takes every element the view shows (at most 300 at a time). Elements are life size, so most are far bigger than a bed. Type a number in Scale 1 : (50 makes a 1 to 50 model) and press Make a scale model: everything shrinks by that one number, so the proportions stay. Life size puts it back. Choose an element in the list, then Lay flat puts the largest flat face that can carry it on the bed, Turn 90° turns it a quarter turn and As designed puts it back the way the model has it. Undo layout takes the last change back. Arrange on the bed spreads everything out. Choose material and quality, connect the printer once, and press Send to 3D printer. A box says the time, material and temperatures first; nothing is sent until you say yes. The layout is a copy: moving things on the bed never changes your model. An element that is not a closed solid (its skin has holes, it passes through itself, or the model gives it no shape) is left off the bed and named with the reason, because a slicer would print it wrongly or not at all. Doors, windows and walls made of several pieces are judged as the one element they are. New in this build, and nothing has yet been printed on a real printer from BimCraft: watch the first prints. A scale model of a building is thin at small scales: look at the preview for walls thinner than your nozzle."}]};
